`,s.jsx(_,{items:[{q:"What does the bottom row of the 3x3 matrix tell you about a transform?",a:"If it is [0 0 1] the transform is affine: the homogeneous divide is by 1, so the map is linear-plus-translation and parallel lines stay parallel. If the first two entries are non-zero the divide depends on position, the transform is projective, and parallel lines can converge — which is exactly what perspective does to railway tracks."},{q:"Why do warping implementations iterate over output pixels and apply the inverse matrix?",a:"Because forward-mapping input pixels does not cover the output evenly. Under magnification some output pixels are never written (holes) and under minification several inputs collide on one output. Iterating over the output guarantees every destination pixel is written exactly once; the price is a fractional source coordinate, hence interpolation."},{q:"You resize a segmentation mask from 512x512 to 256x256 with bilinear interpolation. What goes wrong?",a:"Interpolation averages neighbouring class ids, so a boundary between class 3 and class 5 produces a value of 4 — a class that was never annotated. Label maps must use nearest-neighbour, which only ever copies an existing id."},{q:"Rotating an axis-aligned bounding box by 30 degrees inflates it. Why, and by how much for a 100x60 box?",a:"Because the rotated box is no longer axis-aligned, and re-storing it in that format means taking the axis-aligned hull of the four rotated corners. For 100x60 at 30 degrees the hull is 116.6 x 102 = 11,893 px against the original 6,000 — a 98% inflation. The label now claims a lot of background as object, which is why rotation augmentation is used sparingly in detection."}]}),`
